Rivet installs commands without default key mappings. Configure the editor with `require('rivet').setup(options)`.

## Setup options

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `projects_dir` | `nil` | Parent for new folders; unset uses Neovim's working directory. The destination remains editable. Current-folder creation always uses `:pwd`. |
| `task_output` | `keep` (default) shows completed output; `close-on-success` closes successful final task output and retains it for `:RivetOutput`. |
| `default_preset` | `'debug'` | Preferred choice before a project has a remembered selection. C/C++ uses available CMake configure presets; Python, Cargo, and SwiftPM offer `debug` and `release`. |
| `profiles` | `{}` | Named sets of creation choices. Configured profiles take precedence over saved local profiles with the same name. |
| `identity` | `nil` | Optional file-header defaults. If omitted, the wizard can use an identity explicitly saved with `:RivetIdentity`; otherwise fields start blank. |

For example:

```lua
require('rivet').setup({
  default_preset = 'debug',
  profiles = {
    ['Portable C++'] = {
      language = 'cpp',
      standard = 20,
      modules = false,
      std_modules = false,
      kind = 'app',
      layout = 'feature',
      style = 'LLVM',
      generator = 'Ninja',
    },
    ['C++26 modules'] = {
      language = 'cpp',
      standard = 26,
      modules = true,
      module_layout = 'single',
    },
  },
})
```

These saved creation profiles choose a starting point; every answer is still editable and the complete file plan is reviewed before creation. They do not install tools. The C++ `profile` field selects a workload: `general` creates the ordinary starter, while `cpu`, `openmp`, `mpi`, `cuda`, `metal`, `webgpu`, `vulkan`, and `opengl` add dedicated examples and runtime tests. This workload setting is separate from the name of your saved creation profile.

## Save choices from a project

Run `:RivetProfile` inside an Rivet project, give the profile a name, and approve its preview with `c`. All profiles save `language`; additional fields are:

| Provider | Saved creation choices |
| --- | --- |
| C++ | `standard`, `modules`, `std_modules`, `module_layout`, `kind`, `layout`, `style`, `header_guard`, `generator`, `profile`, and CUDA's `cuda_standard`, `cuda_architectures` when present |
| C | `kind`, `standard` |
| Python | `kind`, `manager` |
| Rust | `kind`, `edition` |
| Swift | `kind` |
| Lua | `kind`, `runtime` |

Project names, destinations, compiler paths, dependencies, files, Git actions, and identity are excluded. The next creation wizard offers your named profiles. Backspace returns to earlier preview questions; text prompts accept `:back`. Back navigation and cancellation do not write a project.

## Remember header details explicitly

`:RivetIdentity` collects optional details, previews styles, and then asks you to apply the local preference. The creation wizard offers **Use saved identity and preview styles**; you can edit or omit those details for each project. Nothing is inferred from your OS account or Git configuration.

The `identity` setup option uses the same `style`, `author`, `email`, `organization`, `created`, `copyright`, `license`, and `description` fields as [file comment headers](/docs/comment-headers/). Ada Lovelace in the examples is a placeholder; new fields are not populated with her identity automatically.

Saved profiles and identity live in `stdpath('state')/rivet/preferences.json`. Remove a named entry or the `identity` property there to forget it. Existing projects retain their own manifest defaults; `:RivetHeaderDefaults` changes those separately.

## Build choices and status

Configure/build/test presets, the Build target, application, and last task are stored per project under `stdpath('state')/rivet/tasks/`. `:RivetStatus` shows them, and the task picker includes them in its prompt. You may use `require('rivet').statusline()` in your statusline plugin to display the active Rivet project choices.

Creation profiles and CMake presets serve different purposes: profiles start a new project, while CMake presets configure and run builds inside one. See [CMake and tasks](/docs/cmake/) for their commands and selection rules.

## Edit clang tool configuration

Run `:RivetConfig` (or `:Rivet config`) and choose clang-format, clang-tidy, or clangd. Each curated key shows its meaning, accepted type, current value, and available choices. Values show their effect before the full file diff is reviewed. The per-check options entry accepts an exact `check.option` key. **Open YAML** opens the underlying file for every option supported by your installed tool, including advanced blocks and conditional clangd fragments. Complex YAML that the guided editor cannot safely preserve is left for direct editing.

```vim
:RivetConfig clang-format
:RivetConfig clang-tidy
:RivetConfig clangd
```

The generated defaults are valid starting points: clang-format inherits the selected upstream style; clang-tidy enables explicit analyzer, bug-prone, performance, and selected modernize checks without making warnings errors; clangd points at Rivet’s selected compilation database. They do not select compiler flags or guarantee that every diagnostic is suitable for your code. Adjust checks after reviewing their results. New C++ files use the current project `.clang-format`.

The guided editor validates with installed tools when available and says when native validation was skipped. Available keys and accepted values depend on the installed LLVM version. The catalog intentionally covers common options; use the complete official references for [clang-format](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ClangFormatStyleOptions.html), [clang-tidy](https://clang.llvm.org/extra/clang-tidy/), and [clangd](https://clangd.llvm.org/config).

```sh
rivet config clang-format
rivet config clang-format IndentWidth 4 --preview
rivet config clang-tidy Checks '-*,clang-analyzer-*,bugprone-*' --preview
rivet upgrade --preview
```

Updating the plugin and [upgrading a project](/docs/upgrading/) are separate steps. Locally edited tool configuration is preserved by project upgrades.
